00:01:45.730 --> 00:01:47.760
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, it does.

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:50.580
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, we
released the Google Cloud

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:52.670
Debugger for Go recently.

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:53.880
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:01:53.880 --> 00:01:58.580
Which is, in my opinion,
a great thing to have.

00:01:58.580 --> 00:02:01.410
I could not have named
that because first time I

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:03.610
heard about a debugger
on production,

00:02:03.610 --> 00:02:07.602
I was slightly scared about
having breakpoints on my--

00:02:07.602 --> 00:02:09.310
MARK MANDEL: Yeah,
that does sound scary.

00:02:09.310 --> 00:02:10.850
I'm just going to
start my processes.

00:02:10.850 --> 00:02:11.140
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:02:11.140 --> 00:02:12.310
MARK MANDEL: But
it doesn't do that.

00:02:12.310 --> 00:02:13.550
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It doesn't
do that at all, actually.

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:16.090
So basically what the
Cloud Debugger does,

00:02:16.090 --> 00:02:19.610
both for Java and Go-- we have
it for both languages now--

00:02:19.610 --> 00:02:22.990
is that for any application that
is running on Google Compute

00:02:22.990 --> 00:02:27.030
Engine, you basically
set it up first,

00:02:27.030 --> 00:02:29.430
and then it will allow
you to add break points.

00:02:29.430 --> 00:02:31.880
But actually, those are
not real break points.

00:02:31.880 --> 00:02:34.490
Those are snapshot points.

00:02:34.490 --> 00:02:36.634
MARK MANDEL: Oh, so it
takes a picture of what's

00:02:36.634 --> 00:02:38.050
going on in your
system so you can

00:02:38.050 --> 00:02:39.595
see what's happening
with your variables

00:02:39.595 --> 00:02:40.930
and your call stack
and things like that?

00:02:40.930 --> 00:02:41.971
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Exactly.

00:02:41.971 --> 00:02:43.700
So you can add some conditions.

00:02:43.700 --> 00:02:46.290
So you don't want to
take any snapshot.

00:02:46.290 --> 00:02:47.930
For instance, you
can say, oh yeah,

00:02:47.930 --> 00:02:51.760
once you take a snapshot of
this line, these four low point,

00:02:51.760 --> 00:02:54.430
the index variable
is 42, and that's it.

00:02:54.430 --> 00:02:55.450
MARK MANDEL: Oh, wow.

00:02:55.450 --> 00:02:56.630
That's actually really powerful.

00:02:56.630 --> 00:02:57.090
That's great.

00:02:57.090 --> 00:02:58.673
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
It's really powerful.

00:02:58.673 --> 00:03:02.627
It will allow you to see
what is the value of all

00:03:02.627 --> 00:03:05.190
the variables in the
scope at that point,

00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:07.180
and also the call stack.

00:03:07.180 --> 00:03:10.320
So you'll be able to understand
not only what you're doing,

00:03:10.320 --> 00:03:11.650
but also how you got there.

00:03:11.650 --> 00:03:12.900
So that's very powerful, yeah.

00:03:13.262 --> 00:03:14.970
MARK MANDEL: So I
haven't played with it,

00:03:14.970 --> 00:03:17.178
but I'm guessing people are
going to be like is there

00:03:17.178 --> 00:03:18.280
a performance issue here?

00:03:18.280 --> 00:03:20.060
Is there something, you know,
I'm running this in production.

00:03:20.060 --> 00:03:20.860
Is that a problem?

00:03:20.860 --> 00:03:22.735
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It's
actually not a problem.

00:03:22.735 --> 00:03:26.580
Taking snapshots will have
a very, very tiny impact

00:03:26.580 --> 00:03:28.180
on your performance.

00:03:28.180 --> 00:03:31.370
But it's actually good enough
that if you have an issue,

00:03:31.370 --> 00:03:33.330
I could definitely not
even think about it.

00:03:33.330 --> 00:03:34.160
Just go for it.

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:35.690
Try it.

00:03:35.690 --> 00:03:38.130
For sure, don't leave it on,
taking snapshots every second

00:03:38.130 --> 00:03:39.780
because it's not really useful.

00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:42.630
But it's something
that it's definitely

00:03:42.630 --> 00:03:45.340
worth the performance cost.

00:03:45.340 --> 00:03:47.190
Because also, the
performance cost

00:03:47.190 --> 00:03:50.050
is only linked to the point
when you actually use it.

00:03:50.050 --> 00:03:52.550
When you're not using it, you
don't have a performance cost.

00:03:52.550 --> 00:03:53.483
So you're good.

00:03:53.483 --> 00:03:54.470
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:03:54.470 --> 00:03:56.030
And obviously, that
performance cost

00:03:56.030 --> 00:03:58.090
is worth it to solve
whatever that issue is

00:03:58.090 --> 00:03:59.046
that you're having.

00:03:59.046 --> 00:04:00.670
And if you have to
do it on production,

00:04:00.670 --> 00:04:02.420
it's probably a
really tricky issue.

00:04:02.420 --> 00:04:03.461
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Exactly.

00:04:03.461 --> 00:04:05.980
It's basically on the order of
nine inches of milliseconds.

00:04:05.980 --> 00:04:07.730
Like I don't know if
it's like around five

00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:08.520
milliseconds or
something like that.

00:04:08.520 --> 00:04:09.080
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:04:09.080 --> 00:04:10.530
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Which
is definitely worth it.

00:04:10.530 --> 00:04:12.946
There's worse things that could
happen to your production,

00:04:12.946 --> 00:04:14.176
really.

00:04:14.176 --> 00:04:16.050
And it's going to allow
you to basically find

00:04:16.050 --> 00:04:19.700
the good values for you
to test on your code

00:04:19.700 --> 00:04:21.120
outside of production.

00:04:21.120 --> 00:04:24.180
And then in there, you can
actually use an actual debugger

00:04:24.180 --> 00:04:26.810
and go step-by-step
understand what's exactly

00:04:26.810 --> 00:04:27.980
the problem with the code.

00:04:27.980 --> 00:04:31.060
Or even better, run a bunch
of unit tests demonstrating

00:04:31.060 --> 00:04:32.440
that your code is wrong.

00:04:32.440 --> 00:04:35.790
Then fix it and fix
your test with that.

00:04:35.790 --> 00:04:36.540
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:37.776
No, that sounds-- yeah.

00:04:37.776 --> 00:04:39.650
Sometimes there are just
those tricky issues,

00:04:39.650 --> 00:04:41.990
like race conditions that
you couldn't bring up

00:04:41.990 --> 00:04:44.140
in unit tests are tricky
to bring up in your tests

00:04:44.140 --> 00:04:46.344
or you didn't think of,
stuff that really, like,

00:04:46.344 --> 00:04:48.260
it only shows up in
production, because that's

00:04:48.260 --> 00:04:50.260
the best place for things
to show up, obviously.

00:04:50.260 --> 00:04:51.270
But these things happen.

00:04:51.270 --> 00:04:53.140
So it's good to have
those sort of tools.

00:04:53.140 --> 00:04:55.860
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It's good that
you mention race conditions.

00:04:55.860 --> 00:05:00.440
It's actually something that
I've been talking lately about.

00:05:00.440 --> 00:05:02.150
So when you have race
conditions in Go,

00:05:02.150 --> 00:05:03.733
one of the best
things that you can do

00:05:03.733 --> 00:05:07.935
is use the data waste
detector, which is as simple--

00:05:07.935 --> 00:05:08.987
MARK MANDEL: Oh, nice.

00:05:08.987 --> 00:05:10.570
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
--is as simple to use

00:05:10.570 --> 00:05:15.741
as when you build your
binary dash, dash race.

00:05:15.741 --> 00:05:16.240
That's it.

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:17.952
That's everything
you need to do.

00:05:17.952 --> 00:05:19.410
And then it generates
a binary that

00:05:19.410 --> 00:05:21.730
will detect any
time that there's

00:05:21.730 --> 00:05:24.189
a race detect-- data race.

00:05:24.189 --> 00:05:24.980
When that happens--

00:05:24.980 --> 00:05:25.920
MARK MANDEL: Yep.

00:05:25.920 --> 00:05:27.669
FRANCESC CAMPOY: --it
will emit a warning.

00:05:27.669 --> 00:05:30.140
So with that, then, you can,
basically every single time

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:32.940
such a warning appears,
you can analyze your logs

00:05:32.940 --> 00:05:35.860
and file a bug or something like
that so that you can fix it.

00:05:35.860 --> 00:05:37.910
If you do that, though,
you have to be careful,

00:05:37.910 --> 00:05:40.510
because it's not like--
it doesn't take snapshots.

00:05:40.510 --> 00:05:42.120
It's actually much slower.

00:05:42.120 --> 00:05:42.870
MARK MANDEL: Oh,
it's kind of slow?

00:05:42.870 --> 00:05:45.078
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, it
will slow down your process

00:05:45.078 --> 00:05:49.484
quite a bit, even though
when there's no data erases.

00:05:49.484 --> 00:05:51.150
Which means that it's
something that you

00:05:51.150 --> 00:05:54.910
might want to do
as a side server,

00:05:54.910 --> 00:05:57.862
like you have a test server that
it's getting maybe some shadow

00:05:57.862 --> 00:05:59.570
traffic from production
and he's actually

00:05:59.570 --> 00:06:00.960
trying to handle those.

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:04.065
And if it takes longer,
it's not really fun

00:06:04.065 --> 00:06:08.047
because it's not impacting
the production traffic.

00:06:08.047 --> 00:06:09.630
It's just getting
some of that traffic

00:06:09.630 --> 00:06:12.516
and if something goes wrong,
files the bug directly.

00:06:12.516 --> 00:06:14.140
So that's also a very
useful technique.

00:06:14.140 --> 00:06:14.850
MARK MANDEL: OK.

00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:15.350
