00:09:42.440 --> 00:09:43.530
You can run it anywhere.

00:09:43.530 --> 00:09:45.810
And actually, in the first
keynote at Dockercon,

00:09:45.810 --> 00:09:48.960
they showed a new developer
starting on their first day.

00:09:48.960 --> 00:09:51.450
All they have to spin
up on their laptop

00:09:51.450 --> 00:09:53.100
is Docker itself
instead sort of having

00:09:53.100 --> 00:09:54.970
to get like the
whole company stack,

00:09:54.970 --> 00:09:56.677
like Python, Ruby,
all that crap.

00:09:56.677 --> 00:09:58.260
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, it's very nice.

00:09:58.260 --> 00:09:58.980
JESSIE FRAZELLE: Yeah.

00:09:58.980 --> 00:09:59.180
It's way easier.

00:09:59.180 --> 00:10:01.346
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, it's
basically getting, like,

00:10:01.346 --> 00:10:04.014
the development environment
inside of the container also.

00:10:04.014 --> 00:10:04.930
JESSIE FRAZELLE: Yeah.

00:10:04.930 --> 00:10:06.430
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That's really cool.

00:10:06.430 --> 00:10:10.010
So I think that rather than
spending time talking about why

00:10:10.010 --> 00:10:11.830
people should move to
containers-- because

00:10:11.830 --> 00:10:14.470
at this point, I'm pretty
sure that most of people

00:10:14.470 --> 00:10:17.920
actually think that that's a
good idea-- I'd like to talk

00:10:17.920 --> 00:10:19.160
about how do you do that.

00:10:19.160 --> 00:10:27.260
So if you were to move a
company from a traditional stack

00:10:27.260 --> 00:10:29.915
to containers, what
could be the first steps?

00:10:29.915 --> 00:10:31.290
JESSIE FRAZELLE:
Yeah, so I would

00:10:31.290 --> 00:10:37.530
say it would be to start taking
apart your monolithic things

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:42.650
into the database, running
that in a container by itself,

00:10:42.650 --> 00:10:44.950
whatever proxy you have
running that in a container.

00:10:44.950 --> 00:10:48.190
So just really separating
out the microservices.

00:10:48.190 --> 00:10:50.980
And then, starting
to play around with,

00:10:50.980 --> 00:10:54.950
maybe, the Docker command line,
and then maybe playing around

00:10:54.950 --> 00:10:56.850
with the new like
platform as a service

00:10:56.850 --> 00:10:59.840
tools, all these
different things that

00:10:59.840 --> 00:11:01.812
have come to fruition.

00:11:01.812 --> 00:11:03.940
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Yeah, last week we

00:11:03.940 --> 00:11:06.510
had an interview
with Brian Dorsey.

00:11:06.510 --> 00:11:08.170
He worked for the
Kubernetes team.

00:11:08.170 --> 00:11:10.480
And he was quite interesting,
because he was basically

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:13.290
talking about, yeah, once you
go through that step of going

00:11:13.290 --> 00:11:17.526
from one monolithic app to
you have, all of a sudden,

00:11:17.526 --> 00:11:20.200
a bunch of containers and
microservices and everything.

00:11:20.200 --> 00:11:22.980
All of a sudden, there's a new
set of problems that set in.

00:11:22.980 --> 00:11:25.250
So basically that's
when you're going

00:11:25.250 --> 00:11:29.480
to be talking about Swarm
or Kubernetes or Mesos

00:11:29.480 --> 00:11:31.106
or whatever stack
that you want to use.

00:11:31.106 --> 00:11:33.438
JESSIE FRAZELLE: And there's
so many to choose from now.

00:11:33.438 --> 00:11:35.380
It's like the world
is at your disposal.

00:11:35.380 --> 00:11:36.650
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
that's really cool.

00:11:36.650 --> 00:11:37.490
It's kind of scary.

00:11:37.490 --> 00:11:39.989
I think that at some point we
should have an episode talking

00:11:39.989 --> 00:11:40.899
about what to do.

00:11:40.899 --> 00:11:42.190
JESSIE FRAZELLE: Yeah. [LAUGHS]

00:11:42.190 --> 00:11:44.023
FRANCESC CAMPOY: What
are the options there?

00:11:44.023 --> 00:11:45.890
I think it's a quite
interesting topic.

00:11:45.890 --> 00:11:48.380
So you mentioned
at the beginning

00:11:48.380 --> 00:11:51.420
of the talk, the beginning
of the interview,

00:11:51.420 --> 00:11:54.120
you mentioned setConf.

00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:56.200
Security is quite a big deal.

00:11:56.200 --> 00:11:59.220
I've seen people putting
private keys inside

00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:02.350
of containers and
on public repos.

00:12:02.350 --> 00:12:03.742
That's kind of dangerous.

00:12:03.742 --> 00:12:05.360
JESSIE FRAZELLE: Yeah.

00:12:05.360 --> 00:12:06.901
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
Tell us a little bit

00:12:06.901 --> 00:12:09.010
about what do you think
that we should always

00:12:09.010 --> 00:12:12.105
take care of regarding
security with containers.

00:12:12.105 --> 00:12:14.230
JESSIE FRAZELLE: Yeah, so
what I'm working on right

00:12:14.230 --> 00:12:16.380
now is, the first
phase is actually

00:12:16.380 --> 00:12:22.600
setConf itself, which is allow
or denying certain sys calls.

00:12:22.600 --> 00:12:26.440
You can actually even
trace, et cetera.

00:12:26.440 --> 00:12:28.600
But the first phase
is actually going

00:12:28.600 --> 00:12:33.240
to be like this kind of
not so easy to use config,

00:12:33.240 --> 00:12:36.470
but not so complex as
something as AppArmor

00:12:36.470 --> 00:12:37.880
or something like that.

00:12:37.880 --> 00:12:40.550
But the second phase
of that is actually

00:12:40.550 --> 00:12:44.290
one config to rule
all security profiles,

00:12:44.290 --> 00:12:47.530
and then it will have different
back ends, like using setConf

00:12:47.530 --> 00:12:50.740
or AppArmor or SELinux label.

00:12:50.740 --> 00:12:55.050
So you get the ability to
log on right on any file

00:12:55.050 --> 00:12:58.830
or deny files being
written to allow

00:12:58.830 --> 00:13:02.075
certain executables to run
like Petri, stuff like that.

00:13:02.075 --> 00:13:02.991
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:13:02.991 --> 00:13:06.060
So basically, the idea is
that, the same way we have like

00:13:06.060 --> 00:13:08.100
with OS/2, you
have scopes and you

00:13:08.100 --> 00:13:10.170
allow people to do one
thing or the other-- it's

00:13:10.170 --> 00:13:12.830
kind of the same thing,
but inside of Linux, right?

00:13:12.830 --> 00:13:13.130
JESSIE FRAZELLE: Yeah.

00:13:13.130 --> 00:13:13.630
Totally.

00:13:13.630 --> 00:13:20.660
It was actually designed and
inspired by the Apple APIs

00:13:20.660 --> 00:13:24.104
and also Android's APIs
for security scopes.

00:13:24.104 --> 00:13:25.020
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:13:25.020 --> 00:13:28.920
Yeah, I saw your demo,
your Linux talk, at not

00:13:28.920 --> 00:13:31.304
[INAUDIBLE], but the
Docker meet-up in Belgium.

00:13:31.304 --> 00:13:32.220
JESSIE FRAZELLE: Yeah.

00:13:32.220 --> 00:13:34.160
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That
was really interesting.

00:13:34.160 --> 00:13:35.750
I wonder if that was recorded.

00:13:35.750 --> 00:13:38.180
If it was recorded,
I'll try to put a link

00:13:38.180 --> 00:13:39.700
to that on the show notes.

00:13:39.700 --> 00:13:40.794
JESSIE FRAZELLE: Oh, nice.

00:13:40.794 --> 00:13:41.710
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Cool.

00:13:41.710 --> 00:13:44.160
So I'm going to say
it again, but you're

00:13:44.160 --> 00:13:48.540
kind of famous for one specific
thing, which is running

00:13:48.540 --> 00:13:50.200
everything in a container.

00:13:50.200 --> 00:13:54.980
Like, I've seen you're running
normal things like Chrome,

00:13:54.980 --> 00:13:58.580
but also I've seen you
even launching games

00:13:58.580 --> 00:14:02.460
from Steam on a container,
which is pretty awesome.

00:14:02.460 --> 00:14:06.100
And it's really amazing the fact
that you're able to do that.

00:14:06.100 --> 00:14:08.370
People tend to say, oh
yeah, that's too hard.

00:14:08.370 --> 00:14:09.990
But then you make it look easy.

00:14:09.990 --> 00:14:14.080
So I wanted to ask you, what
is the next crazy thing you're

00:14:14.080 --> 00:14:14.910
going to do?

00:14:14.910 --> 00:14:18.880
Or if there's no crazy
thing that can disclose,

00:14:18.880 --> 00:14:20.400
what is one crazy
thing that you've

00:14:20.400 --> 00:14:23.100
tried and totally failed?

00:14:23.100 --> 00:14:25.840
JESSIE FRAZELLE: So
my coworker Arnaud

00:14:25.840 --> 00:14:27.750
and I gave a talk
yesterday, and we

00:14:27.750 --> 00:14:33.280
had this crazy idea of fitting
a Docker image onto a 1.44

00:14:33.280 --> 00:14:34.920
megabyte floppy disk.

00:14:34.920 --> 00:14:37.740
And it was all going
to go so perfectly.

00:14:37.740 --> 00:14:41.410
I ordered this floppy
disk to USB converter,

00:14:41.410 --> 00:14:43.440
and I translated the
page from Spanish

00:14:43.440 --> 00:14:46.270
so that I could make sure
that it ran on Linux.

00:14:46.270 --> 00:14:48.190
And I plugged it
in when we got it,

00:14:48.190 --> 00:14:49.890
and it was just
like totally borked,

00:14:49.890 --> 00:14:51.950
like back in the olden
days when you would

00:14:51.950 --> 00:14:53.592
put in your floppy
disk and you just

00:14:53.592 --> 00:14:54.800
knew it wasn't working right.

00:14:54.800 --> 00:14:57.260
It was like the
exact same sound.

00:14:57.260 --> 00:15:01.250
So we were like,
well, [BLEEP], but we

00:15:01.250 --> 00:15:05.210
did create an image that was
small enough to fit on one.

00:15:05.210 --> 00:15:07.820
And the coolest part
about it, actually,

00:15:07.820 --> 00:15:10.590
was that the binary
that was in the image

00:15:10.590 --> 00:15:14.470
was a Go binary built statically
so we could build from scratch.

00:15:14.470 --> 00:15:17.570
And the binary itself
was, like, 5 megabytes,

00:15:17.570 --> 00:15:18.700
which is way too big.

00:15:18.700 --> 00:15:20.770
It's never going to fit.

00:15:20.770 --> 00:15:23.890
But then when you put
it in the Docker image

00:15:23.890 --> 00:15:29.470
itself and you do a Docker
save, it will create a tarball,

00:15:29.470 --> 00:15:30.960
but that tarball
isn't compressed.

00:15:30.960 --> 00:15:34.620
So actually, what we did was
untar it, then retar it with XZ

00:15:34.620 --> 00:15:36.850
and nine compression,
the maximum.

00:15:36.850 --> 00:15:38.344
And it was 1.3 megabytes.

00:15:38.344 --> 00:15:39.260
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:15:39.260 --> 00:15:41.320
So that's actually
very interesting.

00:15:41.320 --> 00:15:43.360
Can you actually
do that in general?

00:15:43.360 --> 00:15:46.530
Once you have that
Docker image untarred

00:15:46.530 --> 00:15:48.090
and then tar it
with compression,

00:15:48.090 --> 00:15:50.540
then the Docker daemon
will be able to handle it

00:15:50.540 --> 00:15:52.030
like if it was a normal image?

00:15:52.030 --> 00:15:53.240
JESSIE FRAZELLE: Yeah.

00:15:53.240 --> 00:15:57.070
So that XZ compressed
image, I could

00:15:57.070 --> 00:15:59.620
have Docker loaded it back in.

00:15:59.620 --> 00:16:02.120
So it actually would be
really nice if on Docker save

00:16:02.120 --> 00:16:05.540
we could pass a flag, like
use this compression level,

00:16:05.540 --> 00:16:09.576
like either Gzip, XZ,
and like what sort of.

00:16:09.576 --> 00:16:11.450
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
that'd be really nice.

00:16:11.450 --> 00:16:13.880
Because nowadays one of
the problems that I have

00:16:13.880 --> 00:16:17.800
is if I try to run
Docker on my machine

00:16:17.800 --> 00:16:21.590
and I have hotel Wi-Fi
or conference Wi-Fi.

00:16:21.590 --> 00:16:24.270
It's kind of a pain because
images tend to be quite big.

00:16:24.270 --> 00:16:25.729
So that could maybe
help with that.

00:16:25.729 --> 00:16:27.020
JESSIE FRAZELLE: Yeah, totally.

00:16:27.020 --> 00:16:28.220
That would be a huge help.

00:16:28.220 --> 00:16:28.770
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That
is pretty awesome.

00:16:28.770 --> 00:16:30.930
The other option, of course,
is using Docker machine.

00:16:30.930 --> 00:16:31.670
JESSIE FRAZELLE: Yeah.

00:16:31.670 --> 00:16:32.970
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Which-- yeah.

00:16:32.970 --> 00:16:36.060
There's actually-- we'll put on
the links one of my coworkers,

00:16:36.060 --> 00:16:39.420
[INAUDIBLE], wrote a
blog post about how

00:16:39.420 --> 00:16:41.899
to use Docker with
the conference Wi-Fi.

00:16:41.899 --> 00:16:43.690
JESSIE FRAZELLE: Oh my
god, that's so good.

00:16:43.690 --> 00:16:45.380
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah, so
basically you started instance

00:16:45.380 --> 00:16:46.850
somewhere like in
Google Cloud platform

00:16:46.850 --> 00:16:48.080
and then you connect to that.

00:16:48.080 --> 00:16:49.040
And it works
really, really well.

00:16:49.040 --> 00:16:50.170
That's what I do
all the time now.

00:16:50.170 --> 00:16:51.700
JESSIE FRAZELLE: That's perfect.

00:16:51.700 --> 00:16:52.533
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK.

00:16:52.533 --> 00:16:55.200
So you mentioned something that
didn't work-- the floppy disk.

00:16:55.200 --> 00:16:56.960
Tell us about something
that you think

00:16:56.960 --> 00:17:00.114
is cool that you've seen lately.

00:17:00.114 --> 00:17:01.030
JESSIE FRAZELLE: Cool.

00:17:01.030 --> 00:17:05.240
Yeah, so actually the stuff
with X server that I do

00:17:05.240 --> 00:17:09.200
is all just mounting
locally my X11 socket,

00:17:09.200 --> 00:17:13.240
but you can also actually
do this entirely remote

00:17:13.240 --> 00:17:14.069
on a server.

00:17:14.069 --> 00:17:18.800
So say you have
Wireshark, which has

00:17:18.800 --> 00:17:21.960
like a GUI running on a server.

00:17:21.960 --> 00:17:25.690
You can have remote
VNC connection

00:17:25.690 --> 00:17:32.150
and actually show it locally
on your local instance.

00:17:32.150 --> 00:17:34.100
So I think that
that's kind of neat.

00:17:34.100 --> 00:17:38.010
And also the fact that
you can-- the difference

00:17:38.010 --> 00:17:42.740
between VMs and containers
is sharing name spaces.

00:17:42.740 --> 00:17:45.760
So the fact that you can
have a Wireshark container

00:17:45.760 --> 00:17:49.170
connected to a
different container

00:17:49.170 --> 00:17:50.900
sharing the same
network name space,

00:17:50.900 --> 00:17:52.810
you can actually look
at all the packets

00:17:52.810 --> 00:17:55.930
from the other container
without exposing everything

00:17:55.930 --> 00:18:00.700
from your host to the
Wireshark container itself.

00:18:00.700 --> 00:18:04.070
So just the fact that Docker
allows you to share .NET

00:18:04.070 --> 00:18:08.740
namespaces, PID namespaces,
it's super helpful in terms

00:18:08.740 --> 00:18:09.560
of debugging.

00:18:09.560 --> 00:18:13.560
And the whole
remote VNC 11 thing

00:18:13.560 --> 00:18:15.680
is also helpful in
terms of debugging.

00:18:15.680 --> 00:18:18.180
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So there's two
really cool things in there.

00:18:18.180 --> 00:18:20.013
One is the fact that--
so what you're saying

00:18:20.013 --> 00:18:22.790
is that you have your containers
that it's running somewhere

00:18:22.790 --> 00:18:26.629
and that it's not aware
of Wireshark or anything,

00:18:26.629 --> 00:18:27.420
just running there.

00:18:27.420 --> 00:18:28.650
You won't have to debug it.

00:18:28.650 --> 00:18:30.410
And without modifying
find that container,

00:18:30.410 --> 00:18:33.050
you're able to start a new
container that will be sniffing

00:18:33.050 --> 00:18:35.050
those packets with Wireshark?

00:18:35.050 --> 00:18:37.060
JESSIE FRAZELLE: Yeah,
so you can actually

00:18:37.060 --> 00:18:39.390
take the network namespace
from the container

00:18:39.390 --> 00:18:43.070
that you want to debug and then
run the next container-- let's

00:18:43.070 --> 00:18:45.640
say your Watershark container,
or like maybe even a strace

00:18:45.640 --> 00:18:47.734
container in that
network namespace.

00:18:47.734 --> 00:18:49.400
And if it was strace,
you could actually

00:18:49.400 --> 00:18:52.620
run it in the PID namespace
of the other container, which

00:18:52.620 --> 00:18:53.540
is really--

00:18:53.540 --> 00:18:54.250
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That's really powerful.

00:18:54.250 --> 00:18:55.130
JESSIE FRAZELLE:
It's fascinating.

00:18:55.130 --> 00:18:57.046
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah,
that's really powerful.

00:18:57.046 --> 00:18:59.810
So basically, you can connect
to that container, the container

00:18:59.810 --> 00:19:01.560
that you want to
investigate that and need

00:19:01.560 --> 00:19:03.360
to know anything you
need to restart it

00:19:03.360 --> 00:19:04.959
or anything to
just connect there.

00:19:04.959 --> 00:19:07.000
JESSIE FRAZELLE: So it
annihilates the whole need

00:19:07.000 --> 00:19:09.570
to like exec in,
install other [BLEEP],

00:19:09.570 --> 00:19:11.500
and then do what you need to do.

00:19:11.500 --> 00:19:13.210
You don't need to exec anymore.

00:19:13.210 --> 00:19:17.000
You can be sure that your
debugging tools won't

00:19:17.000 --> 00:19:19.184
modify the original container.

00:19:19.184 --> 00:19:20.100
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:19:20.100 --> 00:19:22.010
JESSIE FRAZELLE: And, yeah.

00:19:22.010 --> 00:19:25.060
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So that
was one of the cool things.

00:19:25.060 --> 00:19:27.800
The other cool thing
that that implies

00:19:27.800 --> 00:19:32.890
is that you can be running
graphical UIs, QAs, on a server

00:19:32.890 --> 00:19:35.990
and access them like if
they were local applications

00:19:35.990 --> 00:19:37.050
on your laptop.

00:19:37.050 --> 00:19:37.330
JESSIE FRAZELLE: Yeah.

00:19:37.330 --> 00:19:38.288
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Right.

00:19:38.288 --> 00:19:41.090
So that's through X server,
which means that on Linux it

00:19:41.090 --> 00:19:41.840
works really well.

00:19:41.840 --> 00:19:43.520
What about Mac?

00:19:43.520 --> 00:19:45.520
JESSIE FRAZELLE: Yeah,
Mac would be interesting.

00:19:45.520 --> 00:19:50.390
I think it would probably have
to use maybe the same hack that

00:19:50.390 --> 00:19:55.080
is used when people try to
run my stuff on their Macs,

00:19:55.080 --> 00:20:00.860
and it involves a socat into
like an X11 Mac instance,

00:20:00.860 --> 00:20:03.330
and then it fires into to VM.

00:20:03.330 --> 00:20:05.430
I'm not really sure on
the technical details,

00:20:05.430 --> 00:20:08.132
but it's something
like magic. [LAUGHS]

00:20:08.132 --> 00:20:10.090
FRANCESC CAMPOY: That
sounds quite complicated.

00:20:10.090 --> 00:20:12.270
Is there are any
blockbuster something

00:20:12.270 --> 00:20:13.834
that you could point us to?

00:20:13.834 --> 00:20:15.500
JESSIE FRAZELLE: Yeah,
there is actually

00:20:15.500 --> 00:20:18.390
an issue that is closed now.

00:20:18.390 --> 00:20:22.000
But on Docker
itself where someone

00:20:22.000 --> 00:20:23.660
is mentioning MyGUI
apps, and I think

00:20:23.660 --> 00:20:29.850
it's titled like Mac OSX X11,
and the person who figured it

00:20:29.850 --> 00:20:31.270
out posted all of the details.

00:20:31.270 --> 00:20:33.320
And actually, I'll
get emails still

00:20:33.320 --> 00:20:35.824
on this issue of people thanking
the guy who figured it out.

00:20:35.824 --> 00:20:36.740
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Nice.

00:20:36.740 --> 00:20:37.770
JESSIE FRAZELLE:
It must still work

00:20:37.770 --> 00:20:38.981
and it must still be legit.

00:20:38.981 --> 00:20:40.730
FRANCESC CAMPOY: OK,
so I'll check it out,

