00:26:53.450 --> 00:26:55.510
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, so
we have a great question

00:26:55.510 --> 00:26:58.440
of the week from one of
our previous listeners

00:26:58.440 --> 00:27:01.880
and one time answer
providers, a gentleman

00:27:01.880 --> 00:27:04.670
by the name of Terrence
who's a technical solutions

00:27:04.670 --> 00:27:07.070
engineer here at Google.

00:27:07.070 --> 00:27:09.700
He asks a good question, like
a debugging style question,

00:27:09.700 --> 00:27:12.470
which I quite like, which is
sort of this situation where

00:27:12.470 --> 00:27:16.270
it's like why can't my Google
Compute Engine instance connect

00:27:16.270 --> 00:27:20.760
to a particular API resource,
like Cloud SQL of big query,

00:27:20.760 --> 00:27:23.220
or Cloud Datastore?

00:27:23.220 --> 00:27:25.860
And it could be something
that I think has probably

00:27:25.860 --> 00:27:28.510
bitten me a couple times.

00:27:28.510 --> 00:27:30.990
So I think it's an
excellent question.

00:27:30.990 --> 00:27:31.930
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:27:31.930 --> 00:27:34.560
Yeah, I've seen that before
and I've heard people

00:27:34.560 --> 00:27:35.920
asking those questions before.

00:27:35.920 --> 00:27:38.770
So I think it's a great
question of the week.

00:27:38.770 --> 00:27:41.440
So the answer is
actually quite simple.

00:27:41.440 --> 00:27:43.710
It's security.

00:27:43.710 --> 00:27:47.840
By default, when you create an
instance on a computer engine,

00:27:47.840 --> 00:27:51.110
the instance will not have
access to all the things

00:27:51.110 --> 00:27:52.160
that you could do.

00:27:52.160 --> 00:27:55.150
So it will have access
to some of the resources

00:27:55.150 --> 00:27:56.619
that we also call scopes.

00:27:56.619 --> 00:27:58.160
So, for instance,
what you create it,

00:27:58.160 --> 00:28:02.060
it would have read only
access to Google Cloud Storage

00:28:02.060 --> 00:28:04.200
but won't be able to write.

00:28:04.200 --> 00:28:06.700
So what do you do if
you want that instance

00:28:06.700 --> 00:28:07.920
to be able to write?

00:28:07.920 --> 00:28:09.450
MARK MANDEL: So
the easiest way is

00:28:09.450 --> 00:28:12.930
if you're creating an instance
through the development

00:28:12.930 --> 00:28:15.980
through the console,
through the web console.

00:28:15.980 --> 00:28:18.350
If you go to a tab when you
create an instance called

00:28:18.350 --> 00:28:22.220
access and security, you'll see
all the levels of API access

00:28:22.220 --> 00:28:23.010
you have there.

00:28:23.010 --> 00:28:26.730
And as you said, you can
see the Cloud SQL is none.

00:28:26.730 --> 00:28:29.480
BigQuery is none by default.
Storage is read only.

00:28:29.480 --> 00:28:30.640
Because security, right?

00:28:30.640 --> 00:28:32.450
Like you want to keep
things locked down

00:28:32.450 --> 00:28:36.640
and only open up the things
you want to, which is great.

00:28:36.640 --> 00:28:38.100
And that that's
a really easy way

00:28:38.100 --> 00:28:40.870
of saying oh, when I created
that instance before,

00:28:40.870 --> 00:28:43.025
maybe I forgot to turn this on.

00:28:43.025 --> 00:28:44.275
Maybe I should go have a look.

00:28:46.800 --> 00:28:49.320
It's sort of a
very common gotcha.

00:28:49.320 --> 00:28:50.390
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:28:50.390 --> 00:28:52.014
MARK MANDEL: Though,
I do like the fact

00:28:52.014 --> 00:28:53.670
that now there is
a button there that

00:28:53.670 --> 00:28:57.990
says allow API access to
all Google Cloud services.

00:28:57.990 --> 00:28:59.239
That's pretty handy.

00:28:59.239 --> 00:29:00.780
FRANCESC CAMPOY:
That's pretty handy,

00:29:00.780 --> 00:29:05.782
but I could not advise
using it as a general basis.

00:29:05.782 --> 00:29:06.490
MARK MANDEL: Yes.

00:29:06.490 --> 00:29:08.240
FRANCESC CAMPOY: I think
it's very good if you

00:29:08.240 --> 00:29:09.823
want to have an
instance that you want

00:29:09.823 --> 00:29:12.210
to test that your code
is actually correct

00:29:12.210 --> 00:29:14.520
and it's just an access problem.

00:29:14.520 --> 00:29:17.660
Or if you are actually
using that instance

00:29:17.660 --> 00:29:20.970
to do a development
it's a great idea.

00:29:20.970 --> 00:29:25.340
But it's definitely not
a good security policy

00:29:25.340 --> 00:29:29.845
to allow access
to all the scopes.

00:29:29.845 --> 00:29:30.970
MARK MANDEL: Yeah, exactly.

00:29:30.970 --> 00:29:32.844
I think it's a great
troubleshooting feature.

00:29:32.844 --> 00:29:35.850
You know, like let's make
sure that this isn't something

00:29:35.850 --> 00:29:38.660
that the problem
with what's going on.

00:29:38.660 --> 00:29:40.580
And then I can go
back and say OK,

00:29:40.580 --> 00:29:43.060
let's scale these security
measures back and make

00:29:43.060 --> 00:29:44.970
sure everything is
the way it should be.

00:29:44.970 --> 00:29:45.890
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yeah.

00:29:45.890 --> 00:29:49.650
Something interesting than maybe
some of our users will know

00:29:49.650 --> 00:29:53.720
is in the screen that
you were talking about

00:29:53.720 --> 00:29:55.950
before, when you create a
dialog, where you create

00:29:55.950 --> 00:29:58.780
an instance, there's
actually at the button,

00:29:58.780 --> 00:30:01.990
if I remember correctly,
something like equivalent REST

00:30:01.990 --> 00:30:02.880
API?

00:30:02.880 --> 00:30:04.420
Or something like that?

00:30:04.420 --> 00:30:06.190
Or an equivalent GCloud command.

00:30:06.190 --> 00:30:06.900
MARK MANDEL: Ooh.

00:30:06.900 --> 00:30:07.400
Whoa.

00:30:07.400 --> 00:30:08.983
FRANCESC CAMPOY: So
that will actually

00:30:08.983 --> 00:30:12.350
show you how to do the same
thing through either the REST

00:30:12.350 --> 00:30:14.630
API, so it's going
to be a JSON object,

00:30:14.630 --> 00:30:16.080
or with a Gigolo command.

00:30:16.080 --> 00:30:17.900
So that can be quite useful.

00:30:17.900 --> 00:30:21.940
What you want to do is create
that instance with this code

00:30:21.940 --> 00:30:26.872
that you need from a place
other than the console itself.

00:30:26.872 --> 00:30:28.080
MARK MANDEL: Oh, that's nice.

00:30:28.080 --> 00:30:29.907
I'm actually
looking at that now.

00:30:29.907 --> 00:30:31.490
I didn't quite realize
that was there.

00:30:31.490 --> 00:30:33.281
I think I probably
knew, but I'd forgotten.

00:30:33.281 --> 00:30:35.810
But that's like a nice
interactive documentation.

00:30:35.810 --> 00:30:37.560
FRANCESC CAMPOY: It's
a very useful thing,

00:30:37.560 --> 00:30:40.030
because sometimes I don't
remember exactly, especially

00:30:40.030 --> 00:30:43.940
in the JSON formats, like,
oh, where does it go?

00:30:43.940 --> 00:30:47.130
What is the parameter into
GCloud to create this thing?

00:30:47.130 --> 00:30:48.651
So it's very, very handy.

00:30:48.651 --> 00:30:50.150
MARK MANDEL: That's
very, very cool.

00:30:50.150 --> 00:30:51.130
I like that a lot.

00:30:51.130 --> 00:30:53.760
So yeah, if you happen
to run into a situation

00:30:53.760 --> 00:30:55.984
where you think you've
got your code right

00:30:55.984 --> 00:30:57.650
but for whatever
reason you can't access

00:30:57.650 --> 00:31:00.397
Cloud SQL or something like
that from Compute Engine,

00:31:00.397 --> 00:31:02.230
make sure you're going
to check your scopes,

00:31:02.230 --> 00:31:04.715
see if you have access that
way, because you may not.

00:31:04.715 --> 00:31:06.110
FRANCESC CAMPOY: Yep.

00:31:06.110 --> 00:31:06.730
Very good.

00:31:06.730 --> 00:31:11.660
So to wrap up, I would like to
know what are you up to next?

